---

<DocsLayout title="Shell & Remote Access" toc={toc}>

  <h1>Shell & Remote Access</h1>

  <p>
    The command line is your primary interface to polimi-cluster. This guide covers how to connect
    to the login node comfortably and securely, move files around, and keep terminal sessions alive.
    For running jobs, see <a href={`${base}docs/slurm`}>Running Jobs</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- ── SSH Keys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="ssh-keys">SSH Keys</h2>

  <p>
    Password authentication is convenient but weaker than key-based authentication.
    Set up an SSH key pair once and you will never type your password over the network again.
    Run these commands on <strong>your own computer</strong>:
  </p>

  <OsCodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    linuxFilename="local machine"
    windowsFilename="local machine (PowerShell)"
    linuxCode={`# 1. Generate a key pair (Ed25519 is the modern standard)
ssh-keygen -t ed25519

# 2. Install the public key on the cluster
ssh-copy-id USERNAME@LOGIN_IP

# 3. Test key login — no password prompt means it worked
ssh USERNAME@LOGIN_IP`}
    windowsCode={`# 1. Generate a key pair (Ed25519 is the modern standard)
ssh-keygen -t ed25519

# 2. Windows does not have ssh-copy-id — pipe the public key over SSH instead
type $env:USERPROFILE\\.ssh\\id_ed25519.pub | ssh USERNAME@LOGIN_IP "mkdir -p ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys"

# 3. Test key login — no password prompt means it worked
ssh USERNAME@LOGIN_IP`}
  />

  <div class="callout callout-danger">
    <svg class="w-5 h-5 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-red-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
      <line x1="15" y1="9" x2="9" y2="15"/><line x1="9" y1="9" x2="15" y2="15"/>
    </svg>
    <div class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      Keep the <strong>private</strong> key (the file <em>without</em> <code>.pub</code>) on your own computer.
      Never copy it to the cluster or send it to anyone.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── SSH Config ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="ssh">SSH Config & Alias</h2>

  <p>
    Instead of typing the username and IP every time, add a host alias to your SSH config file
    (create it if it does not exist). The IP address is used on purpose: it works even when the
    VPN does not resolve internal hostnames.
  </p>

  <OsCodeBlock
    lang="text"
    linuxFilename="~/.ssh/config"
    windowsFilename={`C:\\Users\\YourName\\.ssh\\config`}
    linuxCode={`# polimi-cluster login node (matteucci-vm-1)
Host polimi-cluster pc
  HostName LOGIN_IP    <-- replace with the login node IP
  User USERNAME    <-- replace with your cluster username
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
  ServerAliveInterval 60
  ServerAliveCountMax 3`}
  />

  <p>
    With this in place, connecting is just:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="bash" filename="local machine" code={`ssh pc`} />

  <!-- ── VS Code & GitHub ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="vscode">VS Code & GitHub</h2>

  <p>
    The <strong>Remote - SSH</strong> extension lets you edit files on the cluster from your local
    VS Code. Once your SSH config is set up, open the Command Palette (<kbd>Ctrl+Shift+P</kbd>) →
    <em>Remote-SSH: Connect to Host…</em> and pick <code>polimi-cluster</code>. Then open your project
    folder via <em>File → Open Folder</em>, or clone a repository with VS Code's built-in Git
    integration (<em>Clone from GitHub</em>), which also handles GitHub authentication for
    <code>git push</code> and <code>git pull</code>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout-warning">
    <svg class="w-5 h-5 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-yellow-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
      <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/><line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
    </svg>
    <div class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      Remote - SSH connects you to the <strong>login node</strong>. Use it to edit code and scripts only.
      Do not run training scripts or notebooks from VS Code's integrated terminal: submit them to Slurm.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Transferring Files ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="transfer">Transferring Files</h2>

  <p>
    Use <code>rsync</code> (or <code>scp</code>) from your own computer to copy files to and from the
    cluster. Both work with the SSH alias defined above. <code>rsync</code> only transfers what changed,
    and can resume interrupted transfers, so prefer it for datasets.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="local machine"
    code={`# Upload a local folder to your private dataset directory
rsync -avP ./my_dataset/ pc:~/dataset/private/my_dataset/

# Download results from the cluster
rsync -avP pc:~/my_project/results/ ./results/

# Copy a single file
scp config.yaml pc:~/my_project/configs/`}
  />

  <!-- ── Navigation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="navigation">Common Navigation & File Operations</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>pwd</code></td><td>Print current directory</td><td><code>pwd</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ls</code></td><td>List directory contents</td><td><code>ls -lah</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cd</code></td><td>Change directory</td><td><code>cd ~/dataset/private</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mkdir</code></td><td>Create a directory</td><td><code>mkdir -p results/run_01</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cp</code></td><td>Copy files or directories</td><td><code>cp -r src/ backup/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mv</code></td><td>Move or rename</td><td><code>mv old_name.py new_name.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rm</code></td><td>Delete files</td><td><code>rm -rf results/old_run/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cat</code></td><td>Print file contents</td><td><code>cat config.yaml</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>less</code></td><td>Page through a file</td><td><code>less train-1234.out</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tail -f</code></td><td>Follow a growing log file</td><td><code>tail -f train-1234.out</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>grep</code></td><td>Search inside files</td><td><code>grep "val_loss" train-1234.out</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>find</code></td><td>Search for files</td><td><code>find . -name "*.pt"</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>du</code></td><td>Disk usage</td><td><code>du -sh ~/dataset/private/*</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-danger">
    <svg class="w-5 h-5 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-red-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
      <line x1="15" y1="9" x2="9" y2="15"/><line x1="9" y1="9" x2="15" y2="15"/>
    </svg>
    <div class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <strong><code>rm -rf</code> has no undo.</strong> There is no trash bin on the cluster.
      Double-check the path before pressing Enter, every single time.
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Useful patterns</h3>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    code={`# Search command history
history | grep sbatch

# Count lines in a file
wc -l labels.txt

# Sort and count unique entries
sort labels.txt | uniq -c | sort -rn

# Extract a .tar.gz archive into a specific directory
tar -xzf dataset.tar.gz -C ~/dataset/private/`}
  />

  <!-- ── tmux ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="tmux">Tmux — Persistent Terminal Sessions</h2>

  <p>
    If your SSH connection drops (because of a network hiccup, a laptop going to sleep, or a VPN
    timeout), anything running directly in your terminal is killed. <strong>Batch jobs submitted with
    <code>sbatch</code> are not affected</strong>: they run on the compute nodes regardless of your
    connection. tmux is still useful on the login node to keep an <strong>interactive</strong>
    <code>srun</code> session, a long <code>rsync</code>, or your editor open across disconnections.
    Detach, close your laptop, and reattach later to find everything exactly as you left it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The key concept: <kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> is the <em>prefix</em>. Press it before every
    tmux shortcut, then release it before pressing the next key.
    A full cheat sheet is available <a href="https://tmuxcheatsheet.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Quick start</h3>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="polimi-cluster"
    code={`# Create a named session (use a meaningful name — your project or task)
tmux new -s debug_session

# Inside the session, start an interactive allocation as normal
srun --partition=debug --time=01:00:00 --cpus-per-task=2 --mem=4G --pty bash

# Detach — the session (and the allocation) keeps running
# Press: Ctrl-b  then  d

# Later, reattach from any SSH session
tmux attach -t debug_session`}
  />

  <div class="callout callout-warning">
    <svg class="w-5 h-5 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-yellow-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
      <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/><line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
    </svg>
    <div class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Clean up after yourself.</strong> An idle interactive allocation left open in tmux still
      holds its CPUs, memory, and GPUs until it hits its time limit. <code>exit</code> the allocation as soon as
      you are done, and kill sessions you no longer need with <code>tmux kill-session -t name</code>.
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Sessions</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command / Shortcut</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Create a new named session</td><td><code>tmux new -s name</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>List all sessions</td><td><code>tmux ls</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Attach to a session</td><td><code>tmux attach -t name</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Detach from current session</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>d</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rename current session</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>$</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Kill a session</td><td><code>tmux kill-session -t name</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Windows & Panes</h3>

  <p>
    A session can contain multiple <em>windows</em> (like browser tabs), and each window
    can be split into <em>panes</em> (side-by-side or stacked terminals).
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Action</th><th>Shortcut</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>New window</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>c</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Switch to window by number</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>0–9</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Next / previous window</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>n</kbd> / <kbd>p</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rename current window</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>,</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Close current window</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>&amp;</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Split pane vertically (side by side)</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>%</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Split pane horizontally (top/bottom)</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>"</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Move between panes</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>↑↓←→</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Zoom pane to full screen</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>z</kbd></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Close current pane</td><td><kbd>Ctrl-b</kbd> <kbd>x</kbd></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Configuration</h3>

  <p>
    <code>tmux</code> is highly customizable. Create a <code>~/.tmux.conf</code> file to set your
    preferred keybindings, status bar, colors, and more. Here's a sample config to get you started:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="~/.tmux.conf"
    code={`# Enable mouse support (click to select panes/windows, scroll history)
set -g mouse on

# Start window and pane numbering at 1
set -g base-index 1
set -g pane-base-index 1

# Increase scrollback history
set -g history-limit 50000

# Faster key repetition
set -sg escape-time 0

# Reload config with: Ctrl-b r
bind r source-file ~/.tmux.conf \\; display-message "Config reloaded"

# Intuitive pane splitting (opens in current directory)
bind | split-window -h -c "#{pane_current_path}"
bind - split-window -v -c "#{pane_current_path}"

# Status bar
set -g status-right '%H:%M %d-%b'
set -g status-style 'bg=#102c53 fg=white'`}
  />

  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Reload config without restarting tmux
tmux source-file ~/.tmux.conf
# Or inside a session: Ctrl-b r`} />

</DocsLayout>
